<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sauerland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sauerland is a hilly, wooded upland region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> valley. Much of it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-arnsberg">County of Arnsberg</a> and from 1368 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a> ruled by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, remaining Catholic. Long known for small-scale iron and metal working, it is now a popular area for hiking and winter sports, around Winterberg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sauerland.jpg" alt="Sauerland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kahler_Asten_(%C3%96senberg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sauerland is a rural, hilly area across most of the south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a> and a small north-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, heavily forested in parts and, apart from the major valleys, sparsely inhabited. Its major rivers are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> and the Lenne, and its highest point is the Langenberg, 843 metres, near Olsberg, just above the better known Kahler Asten, 842 metres, near Winterberg; both belong to the Rothaargebirge. Its bedrock is mostly slates, sandstones and greywackes from the Devonian, when the area lay under a shallow sea, with karstified limestones and caves, the biggest at Attendorn and Balve. The name first appears as Suderland in a document of 1266, and probably means the southern country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1800 the western Sauerland belonged to the County of the Mark, based in Altena, while the eastern part adhered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-arnsberg">County of Arnsberg</a>, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a>, which was owned by the Archbishops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electorate-of-cologne">Electorate of Cologne</a>. The Duchy of Limburg covered a very small area in the lower Lenne valley. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the area passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and was joined to the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, which after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>.</p><p>Parts of the region, especially the major north-western valleys, are an old industrial area. Iron ore, wood and water allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>'s industrialisation. Lead, zinc and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ores supported a mining industry centred on Meggen until the second half of the 20th century, and wire production is still important in Altena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The largest town is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iserlohn">Iserlohn</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludenscheid">Lüdenscheid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnsberg">Arnsberg</a>. Today the Sauerland is the largest tourist region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, with more than 30,000 km of marked hiking trails, the winter sports of Winterberg and Willingen, and six reservoir lakes created to store water for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr area</a>, the biggest being the Möhne and the Bigge. Balve Cave is among the largest prehistoric caves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sauerland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The archbishops turned this title into real rule over the <b>Sauerland</b>, buying the County of Arnsberg in 1368.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a></li></ul></section>
